WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.294 --> 00:00:04.372
At Mr. Brownlow's home, Oliver was immediately put to bed.

2
00:00:04.373 --> 00:00:09.959
Mr. Brownlow's housekeeper sat by the bed, patiently waiting for Oliver to recover from his fever.

3
00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:14.626
But for many days, he remained insensible to the goodness of his new friends.

4
00:00:14.627 --> 00:00:22.436
At last he awoke from what seemed to be a long, troubled dream. Feebly raising his head, he said, "Where am I?"

5
00:00:22.437 --> 00:00:31.373
"Hush, my dear," said Mrs. Bedwin, the old housekeeper. "You must be quiet or you'll be ill again. Lie down."

6
00:00:31.374 --> 00:00:38.611
With that, she gently smoothed his hair back from his face. She looked so kind that he put his hand in hers.

7
00:00:38.612 --> 00:00:43.373
"What a grateful little dear," said Mrs. Bedwin with tears in her eyes.

8
00:00:43.374 --> 00:00:46.770
"What would his mother think if she could see him now?"

9
00:00:46.771 --> 00:00:50.667
"Perhaps she does see me," whispered Oliver.

10
00:00:50.668 --> 00:00:53.333
"I feel as if she's been here with me."

11
00:00:53.334 --> 00:00:55.762
"That was the fever, dear."

12
00:00:55.763 --> 00:01:06.990
"I suppose it was," said Oliver after a moment's silence. "If she'd seen me ill, it would have made her sad. Her face always looks so happy when I dream of her."

13
00:01:06.991 --> 00:01:11.585
Mrs. Bedwin brought Oliver something cool to drink and he fell asleep again.

14
00:01:11.586 --> 00:01:16.419
It was another three days before the fever passed and Oliver was able to sit up.

15
00:01:16.420 --> 00:01:22.308
He was still too weak to walk, so Mrs. Bedwin had him carried downstairs to her own little room.

16
00:01:22.309 --> 00:01:27.982
Propped up with pillows, he sat in a chair next to the fire while Mrs. Bedwin warmed some broth for him.

17
00:01:27.983 --> 00:01:33.804
"Are you fond of pictures?" asked Mrs. Bedwin, who noticed him studying a portrait on the wall.

18
00:01:33.805 --> 00:01:36.653
"I don't quite know, ma'am.

19
00:01:36.654 --> 00:01:43.629
I've seen so few of them. What a beautiful face that lady has. Who is she?"

20
00:01:43.630 --> 00:01:46.304
"I have no idea, my dear."

21
00:01:46.305 --> 00:01:53.383
Mrs. Bedwin handed Oliver a bowl of broth. To him it seemed rich enough to feed a room full of paupers.

22
00:01:53.384 --> 00:01:57.042
He continued to stare at the portrait while he sipped his broth.

23
00:01:57.043 --> 00:02:00.697
"It looks as if she wants to speak to me," he said.

24
00:02:00.698 --> 00:02:07.364
Oliver had scarcely swallowed his last bit of broth when there was a knock at the door, and in walked Mr. Brownlow.

25
00:02:07.365 --> 00:02:14.761
Oliver tried to stand up as a mark of respect for his benefactor. But he was too weak and sank back into his chair.

26
00:02:14.762 --> 00:02:19.689
"Poor boy!" said Mr. Brownlow. "How do you feel, my dear?"

27
00:02:19.690 --> 00:02:25.597
"Very happy, sir. And very grateful indeed for your goodness toward me."

28
00:02:25.598 --> 00:02:27.938
"I'm glad to hear that, Tom White."

29
00:02:27.939 --> 00:02:30.931
"My name is Oliver Twist, sir."

30
00:02:30.932 --> 00:02:36.581
Mr. Brownlow looked puzzled. "But why did you tell the magistrate your name was Tom White?"

31
00:02:36.582 --> 00:02:38.423
"I didn't, sir."

32
00:02:38.424 --> 00:02:42.081
Mr. Brownlow studied Oliver's face to see if he was lying.

33
00:02:42.082 --> 00:02:46.777
And once again he felt strongly that there was something familiar about Oliver.

34
00:02:46.801 --> 00:02:50.249
"You're not angry with me, sir?" asked Oliver.

35
00:02:50.249 --> 00:02:56.152
"No, no . . ." said Mr. Brownlow distractedly. "Look, Mrs. Bedwin, look there!"

36
00:02:56.153 --> 00:03:02.819
He pointed at the portrait and then to Oliver. There was no missing the resemblance between the lady and the boy.

37
00:03:02.820 --> 00:03:08.406
Oliver suddenly felt weak, and as the two adults stared at him, he lost consciousness.

38
00:03:08.407 --> 00:03:15.382
Meanwhile, as soon as Oliver had been captured by the police, Charley Bates and the Artful Dodger ran for home.

39
00:03:15.383 --> 00:03:18.874
"Where's Oliver?" thundered Fagin when they arrived.

40
00:03:18.875 --> 00:03:26.789
The two boys looked at each other but said nothing. Fagin grabbed the Dodger by the collar of his huge coat and began shaking him.

41
00:03:26.790 --> 00:03:33.551
"The police have him. That's all there is to the story," said the Dodger. "Let go of me."

42
00:03:33.552 --> 00:03:38.551
And with one jerk, he swung himself out of the coat, which he left in Fagin's hand.

43
00:03:38.552 --> 00:03:42.789
He snatched up a toasting fork and poked it at the old man's waistcoat.

44
00:03:42.790 --> 00:03:47.186
"Hey!" cried a new voice. "What's going on here?"

45
00:03:47.187 --> 00:03:52.109
A stoutly built fellow entered the room, accompanied by a shaggy white dog.

46
00:03:52.110 --> 00:04:05.442
"What are you up to, Fagin? Maltreating the boys, you covetous old crook? It's a wonder the boys don't murder you. That's what I'd have done if I were your apprentice, helping you fence stolen goods."

47
00:04:05.443 --> 00:04:10.942
"Hush, Mr. Sikes," said Fagin. "Don't speak so loudly."

48
00:04:10.943 --> 00:04:14.665
"You always mean mischief when you call me Mister Sikes."

49
00:04:14.666 --> 00:04:21.503
"Well then, Bill Sikes," said Fagin, anxious to please. "Would you like a drink?"

50
00:04:21.504 --> 00:04:23.924
"Make sure you don't poison it."

51
00:04:23.925 --> 00:04:29.083
Sikes said this in jest, but Fagin gave an evil leer as he poured out the drink.

52
00:04:29.084 --> 00:04:36.329
After swallowing two or three drinks, Sikes looked at the boys again. This led to a conversation about Oliver.

53
00:04:36.330 --> 00:04:45.656
"I'm afraid he'll say something that'll get us into trouble," said Fagin. "And things might come out even worse for you since you're in the business of robbing houses."

54
00:04:45.657 --> 00:04:53.148
"We have to find out what happened at the police station," said Sikes. "You have to get hold of this boy somehow."

55
00:04:53.149 --> 00:05:02.045
Fagin nodded in agreement, but no one in the room wanted to go anywhere near the police station. Fortunately for them, Nancy arrived just then.

56
00:05:02.046 --> 00:05:06.624
"Nancy will go to the police station, won't you, my dear?" said Fagin.

57
00:05:06.625 --> 00:05:09.387
"She'll go," said Sikes.

58
00:05:09.388 --> 00:05:12.704
"No, she won't, Fagin," said Nancy.

59
00:05:12.705 --> 00:05:16.269
"Yes, she will, Fagin," said Sikes.

60
00:05:16.270 --> 00:05:22.792
And Sikes was right. By alternately threatening and bribing her, the men forced Nancy to go.

61
00:05:22.793 --> 00:05:27.458
"Wait a minute, my dear," said Fagin as Nancy was preparing to leave.

62
00:05:27.459 --> 00:05:35.511
He tied a white apron around her waist and gave her a covered basket. "Carry that in one hand; it looks more respectable."

63
00:05:35.512 --> 00:05:43.440
"Oh, my poor little brother!" Nancy exclaimed as she burst into tears. "What has become of him?"

64
00:05:43.441 --> 00:05:47.607
Nancy winked at the men and disappeared out the door.

65
00:05:47.608 --> 00:05:51.011
"Ah, she's a clever one," said Fagin.

66
00:05:51.012 --> 00:05:55.432
Nancy arrived at the police station and went from one cell door to another.

67
00:05:55.433 --> 00:06:00.840
But Oliver wasn't in any of the cells, so she was forced to talk to a police officer.

68
00:06:00.841 --> 00:06:06.332
"Have you seen my brother?" she asked. "He's a sweet-faced little dear."

69
00:06:06.333 --> 00:06:09.420
"The gentleman took him," explained the policeman.

70
00:06:09.421 --> 00:06:14.729
Nancy obtained a few more details about Oliver's release and then ran back to Fagin's.

71
00:06:14.730 --> 00:06:17.737
"We must find him!" said Fagin.

72
00:06:17.738 --> 00:06:28.070
"Charley! Nancy! Dodger! Do nothing but skulk about until you find him. I'll shut up this place tonight. You'll know where to find me."

73
00:06:28.071 --> 00:06:35.650
With that, he pushed them out the door. Then he barred the door and pulled from its hiding place the box that Oliver had seen.

74
00:06:35.651 --> 00:06:42.429
He quickly hid the watches and jewelry underneath his clothing and went out into the city.

